  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThis morning, RAP surface analysis shows expansive high pressure \ncentered over the eastern Province of Ontario at 1027 mb. Light, \nanticyclonic flow dominates over the region; while much of the UP \nshows calm conditions, flow is north-northeasterly where nonzero.\nTemperatures currently range from the low 30s to the mid 50s. \nLocations interior tend to be lower, particularly the west; \nlakeshores remain anchored in the 50s thanks to nearshore lake \nsurface temps near 60F. GOES Nighttime Microphysics shows high cloud \ncover working its way west across the UP, with thicker clouds making \nan appearance near the WI border and across the northern tier. \nLikely this will hinder the downward trend in temperatures across \nthe west, limiting frost formation this morning.\n\nThe Ontario high pressure center deepens and tracks east tomorrow, \nsetting up a Hudson Bay High pattern to the northeast with a Plains \ntrough immediately to the southwest. As the surface high, reinforced \nby mid-level flow, strengthens into Wednesday, the Plains trough \nmoves south toward the Lower Midwest, and clear skies prevail by the \nsecond half of tomorrow. Modeled soundings show deep-layer dry air \nwith substantial (>20C) surface dewpoint depressions both today and \ntomorrow afternoons, and forecast minimum RHs are in the high 30s \nthis afternoon in isolated spots and in the high 20s in the western \ninterior (widespread 30s elsewhere) tomorrow afternoon. That said, \ncritical fire weather conditions will be far from achieved given \nlight winds <10 knots and high temperatures in the low 60s for \nthe next few days.\n\nWith temperature observations tonight quickly outpacing the going \nforecast and dry air and clear skies on the way, efficient \nradiational cooling is anticipated for Monday night. Thus, \nwidespread frost is likely (50-80%) in the western UP Tuesday \nmorning, and patchy frost is possible (20-40%) elsewhere away \nfrom shorelines.\n\nThe split flow pattern slowly moves east of the Great Lakes region \ninto Thursday as high pressure persists over Ontario/Quebec into the \nweekend. Slight variations in LREF clusters make their appearance \nbeginning on Saturday, general consensus exists that some form of \nregional ridging is likely through Sunday. Thus, below-average high \ntemperatures (5-10 below on Monday) gradually warm into above-\naverage high temperatures (5-10 above on Saturday) by the weekend \nwhile conditions remain dry.",
